//ERR any
//MSG="Variable with name inner not found"

// Documented limitation (FEEDBACK.md 1.16): the body of a macro is expanded
// when the macro is *declared*, against the macros known at that point. So
// `inner` is not yet a macro when `outer` is declared, and it survives as an
// identifier. Ordinary use of a macro before its declaration does work — see
// 07_used_before_it_is_declared.cpl — because that expansion happens after the
// whole file has been scanned.

macro outer {
    inner
}

macro inner {
    out "hi"
}

fn main
    outer
